  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does a hot water cylinder cost in Formby?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A vented cylinder replaced starts from £750. An unvented cylinder starts from £1,450, and the price goes up with the size. All prices include VAT.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price, inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Vented cylinder replaced</td><td>from £750</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 150 litres</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 210 litres</td><td>from £1,695</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 250 to 300 litres</td><td>from £1,995</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Change from vented to unvented</td><td>from £1,895</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Immersion heater, vented cylinder</td><td>£175</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Immersion heater, unvented cylinder</td><td>£275</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder yearly service</td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Repairs and fault finding</td><td>£120 first hour (weekdays 9am to 4pm)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is in the price?</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>The new cylinder, supplied and fitted</li>
        <li>All pipe connections, valves and fittings</li>
        <li>Safety valves, expansion vessel and discharge pipe on unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>Testing and setting up before we leave</li>
        <li>Old cylinder drained, removed and taken away</li>
        <li>Building Regulations paperwork for unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Repairs out of hours are £150 for the first hour in the evening, at night and at weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. There is no call-out fee. If a job needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="pressure">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Why is my hot water weak when two bathrooms are in use?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It is almost always a vented, loft tank system. Every tap shares the same low pressure. An unvented cylinder gives mains pressure to every bathroom at once.</p>
      <p>Many Formby homes have 4 or 5 bedrooms and two or three bathrooms. Yet lots still run on the old loft tank system. That is where the trouble starts.</p>
      <p>On a vented system, the pressure comes from the height of the loft tank above the tap. It is low to begin with. Then every open tap takes a share. Run a shower, a bath and the kitchen tap together, and they all slow down.</p>
      <p>An unvented cylinder is fed straight from the mains. Each bathroom gets mains pressure, even when others are in use. No loft tank is needed for the hot water.</p>
      <h3>Before we fit one, we check</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Your mains water pressure and flow, since the cylinder can only give what the mains gives it</li>
        <li>Where the discharge pipe can run safely outside</li>
        <li>That your boiler can heat the cylinder, such as a system or regular boiler</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If your supply is too weak, we tell you straight away. You will not pay for a cylinder that cannot do its job.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="size">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What size hot water cylinder does a Formby home need?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">For a typical 4 bedroom Formby home with two bathrooms, 250 to 300 litres is usually right. Smaller homes may only need 210 litres.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk">
          <thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Good for</th><th>Unvented price</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Size"><strong>150 litres</strong></td><td data-label="Good for">1 to 2 people, one bathroom</td><td data-label="Unvented price">from £1,450</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Size"><strong>210 litres</strong></td><td data-label="Good for">3 to 4 people, a bathroom and a small en-suite</td><td data-label="Unvented price">from £1,695</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Size"><strong>250 to 300 litres</strong></td><td data-label="Good for">4 or more people, two or three bathrooms</td><td data-label="Unvented price">from £1,995</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>This is a guide. Baths use far more hot water than showers. A big bath or a rain shower can push you up a size. Too small and you run out. Too big and you heat water you never use.</p>
      <p>We count your bathrooms and ask how your family uses hot water. Then we pick the size before any work starts. You get the right cylinder first time.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="loft">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a new cylinder for a Formby loft conversion or en-suite?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If the loft tank feeds your hot water, yes. Once the tank goes, the old vented cylinder cannot work. An unvented cylinder fixes this.</p>
      <p>Loft conversions are popular in Formby. Most need the cold water tank taken out, to free up headroom and floor space. A change from vented to unvented starts from £1,895, including VAT.</p>
      <p>This is G3 work, so a general plumber cannot do it. We agree dates with your loft builder so the cylinder goes in at the right stage. When it is done, the work is notified as the Building Regulations require.</p>
      <h3 id="ensuite">Will my hot water cope with a new en-suite?</h3>
      <p>On a vented system, adding another bathroom often shows up the low pressure. We check this at every bathroom survey and tell you if you need an upgrade.</p>
      <p>A new en-suite off the main bedroom is a common Formby project. If you already have two bathrooms, a third can be one too many for a loft tank system.</p>
      <p>Our <a href="/bathroom-fitters-formby">bathroom fitters in Formby</a> can fit the en-suite and the new cylinder together. You get one team, one fixed price and no second firm to manage.</p>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Planning an en-suite or loft conversion?</strong> Call to book a free survey. We will size the cylinder and price it all in writing.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Does my cylinder need repairing or replacing?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Many faults can be fixed, such as a failed immersion heater or a faulty valve. If the tank itself is leaking, it needs replacing.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Often a repair</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>No hot water from the immersion heater</li>
            <li>Drip from a valve or fitting</li>
            <li>Banging or kettling from scale</li>
            <li>Slow to heat, from a faulty thermostat</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Usually a new cylinder</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>The tank itself is leaking or rusty</li>
            <li>You run out when two bathrooms are used</li>
            <li>Weak hot water across the house</li>
            <li>The cylinder is 20 years old or more</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Most cylinders last 15 to 25 years. A planned swap from £750 costs far less than a burst. We find the cause first and tell you honestly if a repair will do.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a cylinder installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We survey for free, give you a fixed written price, then fit the new cylinder. Most swaps are done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Free survey.</strong> We check your space, your boiler and your bathrooms. For unvented, we test your mains pressure and flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed written price.</strong> It covers the cylinder, the fittings, the safety parts and the paperwork.</li>
        <li><strong>Fitting day.</strong> We protect your floors, drain the old cylinder and take it out. Then we fit and connect the new one.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing.</strong> We fill the system, check for leaks and test every safety valve. We show you the controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Paperwork.</strong> Unvented work is notified as the Building Regulations require. You get the certificate and warranty details.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Why G3 matters</h3>
      <p>An unvented cylinder holds hot water under mains pressure. Its safety valves must work every time. That is why only a qualified engineer can fit one. Makers also need proof of a qualified install for the warranty. All our engineers are G3 qualified.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
